`HttpDispatcher.handleData/handleMetadata` expect a context with at least `{ request: ... }` (used for broker call options, auth, etc.), but this example passes `{}`. That can break features that rely on request context (sessions, headers, IP, tracing). Pass the underlying request (or a minimal context object) through to the dispatcher methods.
```suggestion
      result = await dispatcher.handleData(subPath, method, body, queryParams, { request: c.req });
      break;
    case 'meta':
      result = await dispatcher.handleMetadata(subPath, { request: c.req }, method, body);
```

This Edge Function example prepares to accept `PUT` requests (it parses JSON for `PUT`), but the exported handlers omit `PUT`, so Vercel will not route PUT requests to the app. Export `PUT` (and any other required methods) alongside GET/POST/PATCH/DELETE.
```suggestion
export const PATCH = handle(app);
export const PUT = handle(app);
```
